[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m ### Step 3: Use the perpendicular condition
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m We are told that:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m \overline{IA} \perp \overline{OI}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m This is a key geometric condition that relates the triangle's incenter and circumcenter.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m There is a known formula for the distance between incenter and circumcenter:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m OI^2 = R(R - 2r)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m This is a standard identity in triangle geometry.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13765)[0m 
